Assessment — Group Activity, Not Nation-State
Current activity is assessed as consistent with the Group rather than a nation-state actor. Nation-state tradecraft typically presents as low-and-slow — careful persistence, minimal footprint, long dwell times before any overt action. What we are observing is the opposite. Probe activity from external addresses including 109.101.8.2 and 109.107.131.67 has been directed at public-facing infrastructure in the DMZ, with the anomalous appearance of 10.10.200.207 suggesting early-stage reconnaissance outside our expected address space. This activity is concentrated against the web layer rather than attempting to establish footholds deeper in the 172.16.1.0/24 environment, which is consistent with an actor whose objective is impact on or around election day — DDoS, defacement, or a data release — rather than sustained internal access. The timing relative to the election window and the noisy, volumetric character of what we're seeing further supports this assessment.
General Indicators of Compromise
Supporting this, defenders should be alert to volumetric DoS traffic targeting web infrastructure timed around key election milestones, and credential stuffing attempts against publicly accessible portals such as webmail and CMS admin panels using previously breached databases. Defacement or unauthorised content changes on public-facing electoral websites should be treated as confirmed compromise. Social engineering attempts targeting IT staff or electoral workers — particularly calls impersonating technical support or government agencies — are consistent with this Group's methods. Evidence of unusual outbound data transfers from council systems, and any communications on underground forums advertising access to or data from the target municipality, should be treated as high-priority indicators.
